  EnvironmentalTopicDocument(
    number: 55,
    title: "Equipment Maintenance for Noise Reduction",
    definition: "Equipment Maintenance for Noise Reduction is the planned assessment and control of environmental aspects associated with bearings, imbalance, loose guards, exhausts, silencers, lubrication and defect escalation. It connects the activity or material to its source, pathway, receptor, applicable obligation, control owner and verifiable evidence.",
    purpose: "Prevent pollution, nuisance, resource loss and harm; protect workers, neighbours and environmental receptors; comply with applicable approval conditions; and demonstrate control effectiveness. Topic-specific focus: bearings, imbalance, loose guards, exhausts, silencers, lubrication and defect escalation.",
    regulatoryBasis: "Regulatory basis must be confirmed for the actual site and date: UAE federal environmental and waste requirements; applicable emirate authority rules; environmental permit, EIA/EMP commitments, NOCs, municipal/utility conditions and contract requirements. Verify current official instruments and the exact permit condition before assigning legal status. Do not invent universal numeric limits, monitoring intervals, working hours, waste codes or retention periods. Identify each control as legal, permit-conditioned, project-specific or recommended.",
    procedure: "1. Before work: define scope, process, source, pathway and receptor; review legal/permit register, approved EMP, risk/aspect-impact assessment, method statement and emergency arrangements. 2. Set measurable objectives and control selection using the hierarchy: eliminate/reduce at source, substitute or redesign, engineering containment/isolation, administrative planning and training, then PPE only for applicable worker exposure (PPE does not replace environmental controls). 3. Assign competent owner, reviewer, resources, inspection and monitoring plan; brief employees and subcontractors. 4. Start-up: inspect controls, instruments and records; confirm approvals and acceptance conditions. 5. During work: operate within approved conditions, monitor representative conditions where required, record activity/context, maintain controls and inspect after change, abnormal event or complaint. 6. Deviations: stop/modify affected work when control failure, uncontrolled release, permit breach or approved trigger occurs; make area safe, contain/notify, follow emergency and reporting requirements, investigate cause, assign corrective action and verify effectiveness before restart/closure. 7. Review after changes in method, plant, material, location, receptor, weather or authority conditions.",
    documents: "DOCUMENTS & RECORDS — check applicability individually: (a) Environmental Management Plan/aspect-impact register: explains aspects, impacts, controls, owners and review triggers; prepared by environmental/HSE function with activity owners, reviewed/approved per project governance; update before new/changed work. (b) Legal/permit/NOC register: records instrument, authority, condition, responsible owner, due date and evidence; verify against current official source and approval. (c) Risk assessment/method statement/monitoring plan: defines task, locations, parameters, method, competence, triggers and response; approved before activity. (d) Inspection, maintenance, training/briefing, monitoring and calibration records: dated, attributable and traceable to location/activity/equipment. (e) Incident/complaint/CAPA and close-out: preserve facts, notifications, cause, action owner, due date and effectiveness. (f) Contractor authorization, transfer/acceptance or facility evidence where relevant. Classify each as legally required, permit-conditioned, project-required or recommended only after checking the actual obligation. Retention period follows applicable law, permit, client and document-control schedule; do not assume a universal period.",
    documentOwnership: "Accountability matrix: activity supervisor implements daily controls and stop-work response; Environmental Officer maintains aspect/obligation register, advice, monitoring coordination and records; competent specialist designs/validates specialist assessment when required; equipment owner maintains plant; contractor supplies accurate activity and handover evidence; project manager/authorized representative approves formal submissions and resources. Identify named preparer, reviewer, approver, date and revision in the controlled document.",
    verification: "Field verification: inspect the actual source and receptor; confirm controls are installed, suitable, maintained and used; compare monitoring only with applicable approved criteria; check instrument suitability/calibration and competent operator where measurements apply; document date, time, location, operating state, method, weather/context and result. Verify document revision, signatures, attachments, approvals, transfer destination/receipt where applicable, action closure and effectiveness. Escalate missing evidence or nonconformity; retain under approved retention schedule.",
    commonErrors: "Common errors: treating generic guidance as a legal limit; failing to identify receptors or cumulative impacts; starting before approvals/briefing; monitoring at an unrepresentative time/location; not recording operational context; mixing incompatible waste or using an unverified destination where waste topic applies; leaving failed controls in service; closing complaints/CAPA without effectiveness evidence; and not reassessing after change.",
